Title: Software-decode AC-3, E-AC-3 and MPEG-2 with ffmpeg
Origin: Thorium (other/add-ac3-eac3-ffmpeg-decoders.patch and
        other/enable-mpeg2-ac3-eac3-decoding.patch), ported and merged

The audio counterpart to the HEVC patch before it, and the reason that one is
worth having: a 4K HEVC file on the web is usually carrying AC-3 or E-AC-3
audio, and a video that decodes to silence is not much better than one that
does not decode at all. Chromium leaves these to the platform for the same
licensing reasons it leaves HEVC there.

Applies after the HEVC patch and contains no HEVC changes. It does rely on one
thing from it: bswapdsp, which ffmpeg's configure selects for both
hevc_decoder and ac3_decoder, is enabled there.

MPEG-2 video rides along, as it does in Thorium's second patch. It is two lines
in supported_types.cc and the decoder is already in the ffmpeg configs; nothing
on the modern web needs it, but local files and old streams still exist. It is
the one thing in here that is not AC-3, and splitting it into a third patch
would be a two-line patch.

The ffmpeg half.

  third_party/ffmpeg/chromium/config/Chrome/*/*/config.h
                          CONFIG_AC3DSP and CONFIG_FMTCONVERT.
  .../config_components.h CONFIG_AC3_DECODER, CONFIG_AC3_FIXED_DECODER,
                          CONFIG_EAC3_DECODER, CONFIG_AC3_PARSER,
                          CONFIG_AC3_DEMUXER, CONFIG_EAC3_DEMUXER,
                          CONFIG_EAC3_CORE_BSF.
  .../libavcodec/codec_list.c, .../parser_list.c
                          ff_ac3_decoder, ff_eac3_decoder,
                          ff_ac3_fixed_decoder, ff_ac3_parser.
  third_party/ffmpeg/ffmpeg_generated.gni
                          libavcodec/ac3*, libavformat/ac3dec.c, and the x86
                          and aarch64 ac3dsp.

Unlike HEVC, which this series enables everywhere, AC-3 lands in 9 of the 18
configs -- the ones Thorium builds: win/{x64,ia32,arm64},
linux/{x64,ia32,arm64}, mac/{x64,arm64} and android/arm64. The rest keep it
off, which is upstream's state, so no decision of ours is hidden in those
files.

fmtconvert is version skew rather than a mistake in Thorium's patch. Electron
44 pins ffmpeg 2b68d2ba (Chromium 152), where
  ac3_decoder_select="ac3_parser ac3dsp bswapdsp fmtconvert"
and ac3dec_float.c includes ac3dec.c, which calls ff_fmt_convert_init() with no
CONFIG guard around it. Enabling only the first three therefore fails at link:
  lld-link: error: undefined symbol: ff_fmt_convert_init
  >>> referenced by third_party/ffmpeg/libavcodec/ac3dec_float.c
Upstream ffmpeg has since dropped FmtConvertContext from the AC-3 decoder.
Thorium builds against ffmpeg 9db86ce5 (Chromium 156), where
libavcodec/fmtconvert.c does not exist, configure does not select it, and there
is no CONFIG_FMTCONVERT macro at all -- so its patch is complete for its own
tree, and this part is ours to carry until an ffmpeg roll makes it unnecessary.
Whoever does that roll should delete these entries rather than rebase them.

So this patch also sets CONFIG_FMTCONVERT in those 9 configs and adds
libavcodec/fmtconvert.c plus the per-arch init it dispatches to -- x86,
aarch64, arm and riscv, from libavcodec/*/Makefile. All four are needed, not
just x86: ac3dec_float.c is added in the block that covers every Chrome config,
so fmtconvert.c compiles on arm and riscv64 too and wants its
ff_fmt_convert_init_arm / _riscv there.

The AudioToolbox macros (CONFIG_AC3_AT_DECODER, CONFIG_EAC3_AT_DECODER) are
flipped to 1 in every config Thorium touched, including the Windows and Linux
ones. They are inert here -- the GN build takes its sources from
ffmpeg_generated.gni and its registrations from codec_list.c, and neither names
audiotoolboxdec.c or ff_ac3_at_decoder -- so this keeps them rather than
introducing a second kind of difference from Thorium to maintain.

The media half.

  media/base/supported_types.cc
                          IsDecoderDolbyAc3Eac3Supported answers yes when
                          ffmpeg is built, ahead of the mojo/platform path that
                          otherwise asks a hardware decoder. kMPEG2 moves out
                          of the unconditional-false arm of
                          IsDefaultDecoderSupportedVideoType and returns true.
  media/ffmpeg/ffmpeg_common.cc
                          AudioCodecToCodecID maps kAC3 and kEAC3 to their
                          AVCodecIDs, without which the decoder is selected and
                          then handed AV_CODEC_ID_NONE, and
                          GetAllowedAudioDecoders gains ",ac3,eac3". Plus the
                          sample format, below.

The sample format, which is why AC-3 only *looked* like it worked. Upstream
builds the AC3/EAC3 demuxers without the decoders and passes the bitstream to a
platform decoder, so AVCodecContextToAudioDecoderConfig hardcodes
  sample_format = kSampleFormatS16
("the sample_format for AC3/EAC3 must be 16", ETSI TS 102 366 F.3.1 and F.5.1 --
true of the bitstream, not of decoded audio) and then returns early, before the
check that bytes_per_channel agrees with ffmpeg. Build the decoder and ffmpeg
reports its real output, planar float from ff_ac3_decoder, while the config
still says 16-bit interleaved: the stream demuxes, a decoder is chosen, frames
come out, and what describes them is wrong. Both sites are now conditional on
ffmpeg having left sample_fmt unset, so a demux-only build keeps upstream's
behaviour exactly and a build with the decoder gets the truth. Reported to
Thorium, which has the same problem for the same reason.
  media/filters/ffmpeg_glue.cc
                          the ac3 and eac3 demuxers, so a bare .ac3 or .eac3
                          stream is openable and not only AC-3 inside MP4.

The AC-3 paths stay behind ENABLE_PLATFORM_AC3_EAC3_AUDIO, as upstream has
them, so this does not reach into builds that do not ask for those codecs.

Which means this half of the patch does nothing at all without
  enable_platform_ac3_eac3_audio = true
in the GN args, and nothing warns you: Chromium defaults that to cast devices
and tvOS, and with it off mime_util never adds AC3/EAC3 to the mp4 and mkv
codec sets, CodecIDToAudioCodec cannot name the codec, and every hunk below in
media/ compiles to nothing while ffmpeg still carries the decoders. What that
looks like from the page is isTypeSupported() returning false and
MediaCapabilities logging "Failed to parse audio contentType". assets/args/
sets it; a build that imports its own args needs to as well.

Verified by building third_party/ffmpeg:ffmpeg for win/x64 with both patches
applied: `ninja -t inputs ffmpeg.dll` then lists ac3dec_float, ac3dec_fixed,
ac3dec, ac3dsp, ac3tab, ac3, ac3dec_data, eac3dec, eac3_data, fmtconvert and
fmtconvert_init alongside the hevc objects. The media half compiles:
supported_types, ffmpeg_common, ffmpeg_video_decoder and ffmpeg_glue.

